Later
=====

A Later[T] is the result of something in the world that ends later: the end of a process, the next input file, the answer of an IQL dialog.

    Later: Sealed{}
    Later[T:*]: Later{
      mut .await: T;
      mut .awaitUpTo(time: Duration): mut Opt[T];
      }
    Laters: Sealed{
      .first[R:**](laters: mut List[mut Later], callbacks: mut List[mut MF[R]]): R;
      .firstUpTo[R:*](time: Duration, laters: mut List[mut Later], callbacks: mut List[mut MF[R]]): mut Opt[R];
      }

    CapRunningMain[R]:{ ... mut .end: mut Later[R]; mut .terminate: Void; }
    CapInputCursor:{ ... mut .file: mut Later[mut CapInputCursorNode]; mut .next: mut CapInputCursor; }
    CapIql:{ ... mut .ask(query: Query): mut Later[Answer]; }    (IqlDesign)

.end and .file replace CapRunningMain.await, CapRunningMain.awaitUpTo and CapInputCursor#; CapProcess.run is .start, then .end.await.

A Later gets its result once, when what it waits for ends: a value, or an error.
A Later made after that end has its result already.
From then on, every call on the Later gives that result: the same value, or the same error thrown again.
.await blocks until the Later has its result, then returns the value or throws the error.
.awaitUpTo(time) is .await, but gives an empty Opt after time; with Duration#0 it only checks.
Laters.first blocks until a Later of laters has its result, and takes the first such Later of the list:
it throws the error, or it runs the callback at the same index once and returns what the callback returns.
No other callback runs; the other Laters keep their results for later calls.
Laters.firstUpTo is Laters.first, but gives an empty Opt after time.
Both throw a deterministic error before waiting when laters is empty, or when laters and callbacks have different sizes.
A wait that can never end throws a non deterministic error: a wait on a Later of CapInputCursor.file whose file has not arrived, with no window of the program open.

    .let build= {sys.process.start(Build, {}).end}
    .let answer= {sys.iql.ask(SettingsQuery)}
    .let msg= {Laters.first(Lists#[mut Later](build, answer), Lists#[mut MF[Str]](
      {"built"},
      {"answered: "+(answer.await.str)}
      ))}

Implementation notes
====================

- Laters.first waits on the runtime objects of its Laters at once, with no polling: the Later of a Revokable wrapper is a runtime object made around the Later it wraps, checking the Revocation at each call.
